---
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import PageContents, { type ContentsGroup } from '../components/PageContents.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import ScoreGrid from '../components/ScoreGrid.astro';
import { references } from '../data/references';
import alphaWarm from '../assets/photos/alpha-warm.jpg';
import conceptHero from '../assets/photos/concept-hero.jpg';
import teapotWaterCycle from '../assets/photos/teapot-water-cycle.jpg';

const facets = [
  {
    title: 'A fundamental human need',
    text: 'Belonging is a universal drive for lasting, meaningful relationships (Baumeister & Leary, 1995; Allen, 2020). As Kelly-Ann Allen writes, "our need to belong is like our need for water: we can spend some time ignoring our thirst, but sooner or later, our body will start to suffer." It sits right after food, water, and safety in Maslow’s hierarchy—felt as being accepted, valued, and understood.',
    cite: 'Baumeister & Leary, 1995; Allen, 2020',
  },
  {
    title: 'A process, not a state',
    text: 'Belonging is never fixed. It flickers, depends on context, and is renegotiated continuously. Because it is shaped by personal autonomy, choice, and commitment, belonging can never be forced or imposed.',
    cite: 'Allen, 2020',
  },
  {
    title: 'Built in small acts',
    text: 'Shared moments—listening, affirming, moving in time, or sharing a thought—build connection step by step. Reframing shared struggles as normal helps ensure difficulty doesn’t feel like proof that you don’t fit in (Walton & Cohen, 2011).',
    cite: 'Walton & Cohen, 2011',
  },
  {
    title: 'Quality over quantity',
    text: 'What matters is frequent, warm, stable contact grounded in mutual care. Moving from zero close relationships to two transforms wellbeing; going from twenty to twenty-two makes very little difference.',
    cite: 'Baumeister & Leary, 1995',
  },
  {
    title: 'Embodied and placed',
    text: 'Belonging lives in the body, attaching to places, objects, sounds, and textures. Think of a space where you truly belong, and every sensory memory returns all at once.',
    cite: 'Allen, 2020',
  },
  {
    title: 'There even in absence',
    text: 'Even when the original setting is gone, belonging leaves a deep imprint: shared cultural values, implicit norms, and subtle social bonds. Though modern life often treats us as isolated individuals, we remain deeply enmeshed with the world around us.',
    cite: 'Reflection',
  },
];

const belongingMap = [
  {
    quality: 'Embodied belonging',
    choice: 'A multi-sensory space',
    how: 'Belonging is anchored in the body. Settling beneath the cloud on soft beanbags, feeling its texture, watching light warm surrounding surfaces, and sensing sudden lightning flashes grounds attention into the present moment.',
  },
  {
    quality: 'Belonging is a journey',
    choice: 'Clear transitions & shared time',
    how: 'An audio-visual arc guides the experience: thematic musical scores mark key shifts, light dynamics evolve over time, and the familiar cycle of sky and daylight provides a shared temporal frame.',
  },
  {
    quality: 'Belonging is dynamic',
    choice: 'Biofeedback',
    how: 'A sensor follows your breathing, gently altering the cloud’s illumination. Breath flows through the light as a seamless continuation of your body, creating a tangible sense of connection.',
  },
  {
    quality: 'Playing a role over control',
    choice: 'Co-existence & mutual presence',
    how: 'Inter-being is not one-way control. Rather than pressing buttons to command an effect, your breath illuminates the cloud with the sky’s natural light—turning an automatic bodily rhythm into a shared, atmospheric role.',
  },
  {
    quality: 'Self-contained object in space',
    choice: 'Object-based lighting',
    how: 'Embedded internal lighting radiates outward in all directions. With no stage, screen, or audience partition, participants can gather anywhere in the room as equal inhabitants under the same sky.',
  },
  {
    quality: 'Carrying the experience with you',
    choice: 'Subtle transitions home',
    how: 'The magical arc is framed between everyday states (dusk and daylight). Subtle audio fades and light transitions create continuity, helping the feeling of the installation linger long after returning to daily life.',
  },
];

const whyCloud = [
  {
    title: 'It is above everyone',
    text: 'Under a large sky, nobody has a better seat than another. A cloud offers a shared perspective without an explicit stage.',
  },
  {
    title: 'It gives light a body while hiding internals',
    text: 'Clouds are largely opaque, yet they allow light to penetrate and diffuse through their outer layers. This makes the cloud’s surface a natural diffuser for embedded lighting, while concealing the electronics and supporting structures within. By controlling how light spreads through the material, we can recreate the appearance of light passing through a cloud, without making the structure itself transparent.',
  },
  {
    title: 'It is always changing',
    text: 'Though our structure is static, the play of light on it changes and particular states like whether there is a lightning storm or not gives it dynamics. We did consider whether we could add physical dynamic aspects to the cloud to make it slowly morph shape but we decided it would be too complex. We also considered carefully modulating lighting from inside to make it appear like the cloud was changing shape. This might be possible with many more lights and a more uniform surface but it maybe have made the cloud appear like a backlit projector screen. Picture a spheroid with some mapped projectors inside it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'It is water, and so are we',
    text: 'As mentioned before clouds are a powerful symbol in the teachings of Thich Nhat Hanh related to inter-being. We only played with this idea by having tea in some of our sessions but we could have taken this further.',
  },
  {
    title: 'It follows you home',
    text: 'Everyone sees clouds. We hope that people can reflect on the purpose of installation when they next look up in the sky. We are interested in transporting this experience to other contexts and leaning in on this is one way in which that might be accomplished.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Reality in a bottle',
    text: 'The cloud is lit from inside and so we can remove the outside lighting. This gives it the surreal impression of it having been captured from a different context and implanted into our space and serving as our soul point of focus. Being able to turn off the lights also allows us to hide imperfection in the space. Having said that, the light emitted from the cloud has power, particularly with lightning flashes.',
  },
];

const decisions = [
  {
    choice: 'Breath as the interface',
    why: 'Breath is both voluntary and involuntary, and everyone has it. Breathing in rhythm with something is one of the simplest forms of co-regulation. It also builds on what Zéphir learned from Réespiration.',
  },
  {
    choice: 'Breathing with the cloud, not controlling it',
    why: 'We want people to feel that they belong with the cloud, not that they are in charge of it. Its light follows the rise and fall of your respiration, like a companion keeping time with you, rather than obeying commands.',
  },
  {
    choice: 'Lightning at dusk',
    why: 'People arrive carrying their day. As the sun sets, the first lightning pulls attention into the room and towards weather: nature as vast, a little wild, and very present.',
  },
  {
    choice: 'A magical twilight before the breath',
    why: 'The twilight turns the experience into a story. As its light intensifies and colours flare up through the whole cloud at once, the cloud gains a presence and a character. By the time it begins to breathe with you, a bond is already there.',
  },
  {
    choice: 'Lying down, shoes off',
    why: 'Posture changes the role you play. Lying on the floor under a sky turns a visitor into an inhabitant, and the small ritual of preparation already begins the shared experience.',
  },
  {
    choice: 'No instructions about feelings',
    why: 'Being told to feel connected is the fastest way not to be. The space offers conditions and a little mystery, and leaves the story to the people in it.',
  },
];

const openQuestions = [
  'How do we invite someone to breathe with the cloud without instructing them?',
  'How much of the twilight’s story should be told, and how much left to the imagination?',
  'How does the bond with the cloud carry on after you leave?',
  'Where could it live next: a gallery, a public space, a train carriage, a boat?',
  'What would a space for belonging to each other, or to ourselves, look like?',
];

const themes = [...new Set(references.map((r) => r.theme))];

const glow = 'stroke="url(#toc-glow)"';
const tones = { belonging: '#33bbff', design: '#00aaff', bibliography: '#c44f99' };

const contents: ContentsGroup[] = [
  {
    id: 'part-belonging',
    title: 'Our slice of belonging',
    tone: tones.belonging,
    labelColor: '#0d8ece',
    previewAlpha: 0.29,
    gradient: { m1: 54, m2: 57, m3: 28, p1: 0, p2: 48, p3: 54, glow: 0.47, glowX: 16, glowY: 51, c1: '#2d9bd2', c2: '#54f8f5', c3: '#00eeff' },
    items: [
      {
        id: 'what',
        label: 'What belonging means',
        icon: `<circle cx="24" cy="24" r="14"/><path ${glow} d="M17 25c3 4 11 4 14 0"/><circle cx="19" cy="20" r="1.4" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"/><circle cx="29" cy="20" r="1.4" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"/>`,
      },
      {
        id: 'meaning',
        label: 'Belonging and nature',
        icon: `<rect x="12" y="20" width="24" height="18" rx="2"/><path ${glow} d="M8 20L24 8l16 12"/><path d="M20 38V28h8v10"/>`,
      },
      {
        id: 'interbeing',
        label: 'Interbeing',
        icon: `<path d="M13 22h22a6 6 0 0 0 .5-12A9 9 0 0 0 18 8a7 7 0 0 0-5 14Z"/><path ${glow} d="M18 27v4M24 27v6M30 27v4"/><path d="M14 36h20l-2 6H16Z"/>`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'part-design',
    title: 'Installation design',
    tone: '#ffae00',
    labelColor: '#9b2f16',
    previewAlpha: 0.29,
    gradient: { m1: 24, m2: 36, m3: 33, p1: 0, p2: 27, p3: 70, glow: 0, glowX: 64, glowY: 67, c1: '#00e1ff', c2: '#ff8800', c3: '#ff2600' },
    items: [
      {
        id: 'into-a-room',
        label: 'Into a room',
        icon: `<path d="M8 40V18L24 8l16 10v22"/><path ${glow} d="M16 26h16a4 4 0 0 0 0-8 6 6 0 0 0-11-1 5 5 0 0 0-5 9Z"/>`,
      },
      {
        id: 'narrative',
        label: 'The narrative',
        icon: `<path d="M6 34h36"/><path ${glow} d="M14 34a10 10 0 0 1 20 0"/><path d="M30 12a7 7 0 1 0 6 10 6 6 0 0 1-6-10Z"/>`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'part-bibliography',
    title: 'Bibliography',
    tone: tones.bibliography,
    previewAlpha: 0.29,
    gradient: { m1: 33, m2: 27, m3: 25, p1: 3, p2: 20, p3: 65, glow: 0.62, glowX: 10, glowY: 100, c1: '#ff2500', c2: '#c800ff', c3: '#3366ff' },
    items: [
      {
        id: 'references',
        label: 'What we read',
        icon: `<path d="M10 10h12a4 4 0 0 1 4 4v24a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H10Z"/><path ${glow} d="M38 10H26v28a3 3 0 0 1 3-3h9Z"/>`,
      },
    ],
  },
];

const partStop = (group: ContentsGroup, stop: '1' | '3') => {
  const gradient = group.gradient ?? {};
  const colour = gradient[`c${stop}`] ?? group.tone;
  const mix = gradient[`m${stop}`] ?? (stop === '1' ? 10 : 30);
  return `color-mix(in srgb, ${colour} ${mix}%, var(--page-paper))`;
};

const partStyle = (group: ContentsGroup, previous?: ContentsGroup) => {
  const gradient = group.gradient ?? {};
  return [
    `--tone:${group.tone}`,
    group.labelColor ? `--part-label-color:${group.labelColor}` : '',
    `--prev-stop:#fff`,
    `--c1:${gradient.c1 ?? group.tone}`,
    `--c2:${gradient.c2 ?? group.tone}`,
    `--c3:${gradient.c3 ?? group.tone}`,
    `--m1:${gradient.m1 ?? 10}%`,
    `--m2:${gradient.m2 ?? 18}%`,
    `--m3:${gradient.m3 ?? 30}%`,
    `--p1:${gradient.p1 ?? 0}%`,
    `--p2:${gradient.p2 ?? 45}%`,
    `--p3:${gradient.p3 ?? 100}%`,
    `--part-p1:${((gradient.p1 ?? 0) / 100) * 34}rem`,
    `--part-p2:${((gradient.p2 ?? 45) / 100) * 34}rem`,
    `--part-p3:${((gradient.p3 ?? 100) / 100) * 34}rem`,
    `--glow:${gradient.glow ?? 0.45}`,
    `--glow-x:${gradient.glowX ?? 85}%`,
    `--glow-y:${gradient.glowY ?? 30}%`,
  ].join(';');
};

const questions = [
  'How do you feel that you belong without feeling that you are being controlled?',
  'What is the difference between having control and playing a role?',
  'Can a room of strangers become a “we” in half an hour — and should it?',
  'Can we belong to weather? To a sky, a cloud, a season?',
  'How do you design for connection that is felt, not performed?',
  'What trace can a one-off encounter leave, so that people might meet again?',
  'When everyone breathes together, where does my breath end?',
];
---

<Base title="Concept" description="The concept behind Cloudfulness: belonging as a need, a process and an embodied experience, why art and research-creation, and how the ideas became a cloud.">
  <div class="page-wash page-wash-concept">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="Concept"
      image={conceptHero}
      imageAlt="A hand reaching up inside the cloud’s white mesh shell, showing bamboo skewers holding the layers together during assembly."
      title="Belonging, inter-being and a cloud"
      lede="How can art and technology create sublime moments that stay with you? In Cloudfulness, we explored the idea of emitting a moment of heightened sensory stimulation and a hallucination-like feeling of inter-being with a cloud. This was framed inside a mystical environmental narrative arc that peaks during the dawn before dissolves back into daylight."
    />
  </div>

  <PageContents groups={contents} />

  <div class="part" id="part-belonging" style={partStyle(contents[0])}>
    <header class="part-head">
      <div class="wrap reveal">
        <p class="part-num">Part 1</p>
        <h2>Our slice of belonging</h2>
        <p class="lede measure">What belonging means to us, how it reaches beyond people, and cloudful inter-being.</p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <p class="part-tag" aria-hidden="true"><span>Part 1 · Our slice of belonging</span></p>

  <section class="section" id="what">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Five facets</p>
        <h2>What belonging means</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="facet-grid facet-grid-static">
        {
          facets.map((f) => (
            <li class="reveal">
              <article class="facet-card facet-card-static">
                <h3>{f.title}</h3>
                <p class="facet-text">{f.text}</p>
                <p class="facet-cite">{f.cite}</p>
              </article>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="meaning">
    <div class="wrap grid-2">
      <div class="reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Outside of social connection</p>
        <h2>Belonging and nature</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <p>
          Socially speaking, belonging is the felt sense of being accepted, included, understood, welcomed, liked,
          and appreciated. But belonging extends beyond our relationships with others. It is also about how we
          relate to ourselves, how comfortable we feel in our own skin, and how connected we feel to the world
          around us.
        </p>
        <p>
          To that extent it's not only about being accepted by others, but also about feeling at home in ourselves and
          in the world. It provides a sense of safety and rootedness from which we can explore, engage with, and
          experience the world more openly. In this way, belonging is more than a social need; it is a foundation
          for curiosity, openness, exploration, and meaningful connection.
        </p>
        <p>
          When your social sense of connection is damaged, your only moments of solace might come from your connection
          to the world. Nature provides a neutral and primal point of reconnection.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wash interbeing" id="interbeing">
    <div class="wrap grid-2">
      <div class="reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Inter-being</p>
        <h2>Interbeing: there is a cloud in your tea</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="https://plumvillage.org/about/thich-nhat-hanh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thich Nhat Hanh</a>,
          the late Vietnamese Zen master and peace activist, often pointed to a cup of tea and saw a cloud in it. The
          cloud has not died: it became rain, then a river, then this tea. Nothing is only itself. It
          <em>inter-is</em> with everything that made it possible. It was from this concept of inter-being that we drew
          our core inspiration for Cloudfulness, choosing a breathing cloud as the physical medium of our
          installation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="button" href={url('/installation/')}>See how the installation puts this into practice</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src={teapotWaterCycle}
        alt="A dreamy watercolor illustration of a teapot with steam rising to form a beautiful landscape showing the water cycle: a rain cloud, flowing river, mountains, and floating blossoms."
        widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
        sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 26rem, 100vw"
        class="reveal"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  </div>

  <div class="part" id="part-design" style={partStyle(contents[1], contents[0])}>
    <header class="part-head">
      <div class="wrap reveal">
        <p class="part-num">Part 2</p>
        <h2>Installation design</h2>
        <p class="lede measure">Designing a room with a cloud and a narrative experience for interbeing.</p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <p class="part-tag" aria-hidden="true"><span>Part 2 · Installation design</span></p>

  <section class="section built-in" id="into-a-room">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Design features of belonging</p>
        <h2>Building inter-being into a room</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="facet-grid">
        {
          belongingMap.map((m) => (
            <li class="reveal">
              <details class="facet-card" name="design-feature">
                <summary>
                  <div class="facet-head">
                    <p class="facet-eyebrow">{m.choice}</p>
                    <h3>{m.quality}</h3>
                  </div>
                  <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                </summary>
                <div class="facet-body"><p>{m.how}</p></div>
              </details>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ScoreGrid />

  <section class="section wash" id="why-a-cloud">
    <div class="wrap why-cloud">
      <div class="why-text">
        <div class="section-head reveal">
          <p class="eyebrow">Some cloud-related practical and thematic considerations</p>
          <h2>Something we all live under</h2>
        </div>
        <ul class="facet-grid facet-grid-two">
          {
            whyCloud.map((w) => (
              <li class="reveal">
                <details class="facet-card" name="cloud-aspect">
                  <summary>
                    <div class="facet-head">
                      <h3>{w.title}</h3>
                    </div>
                    <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="facet-body"><p>{w.text}</p></div>
                </details>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src={alphaWarm}
        alt="Looking up from the floor at the fibre cloud glowing warm white, hanging from the ceiling in a dark room."
        caption="Looking up at the pilot cloud, 29 July 2026."
        ratio="3 / 4"
        widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
        sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 22rem, 100vw"
        class="reveal why-video"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  </div>

  <div class="part" id="part-bibliography" style={partStyle(contents[2], contents[1])}>
    <header class="part-head">
      <div class="wrap reveal">
        <p class="part-num">Part 3</p>
        <h2>Bibliography</h2>
        <p class="lede measure">The literature behind the concept.</p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <p class="part-tag" aria-hidden="true"><span>Part 3 · Bibliography</span></p>

  <section class="section references" id="references">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Sources</p>
        <h2>What we read</h2>
        <p class="measure">
          A selection from our literature review. These readings shaped our thinking about belonging, group practice,
          ritual and interactive experience.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="refs">
        {
          themes.map((theme) => (
            <div class="theme reveal">
              <h3>{theme}</h3>
              <ul>
                {references
                  .filter((r) => r.theme === theme)
                  .map((r) => (
                    <li>{r.url ? <a href={r.url}>{r.citation}</a> : r.citation}</li>
                  ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  :global(main) {
    --page-paper: var(--paper);
  }
  .part {
    --part-wave-depth: 6.25rem;
    --part-stop-1: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c1) var(--m1), var(--page-paper));
    --part-stop-2: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c2) var(--m2), var(--page-paper));
    --part-stop-3: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c3) var(--m3), var(--page-paper));
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    --paper: #fff;
    background:
      radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 70% at var(--glow-x) var(--glow-y), rgb(255 255 255 / var(--glow)), transparent 72%),
      linear-gradient(180deg, var(--part-stop-1) var(--part-p1), var(--part-stop-2) var(--part-p2), var(--part-stop-3) var(--part-p3), #fff 42rem, #fff 100%);
    padding-block: 6rem;
  }
  .part::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    height: var(--part-wave-depth);
    z-index: 2;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: var(--prev-stop);
    -webkit-mask: var(--wave) 0 0 / 100% 100% no-repeat;
    mask: var(--wave) 0 0 / 100% 100% no-repeat;
  }
  .part::before {
    top: 0;
  }
  .part :global(.wash) {
    --wash-tint: var(--tone);
    --wash: color-mix(in srgb, transparent 55%, var(--tone));
    --wash-deep: color-mix(in srgb, transparent 70%, var(--tone));
    background:
      radial-gradient(ellipse 60% 75% at 78% 45%, rgb(255 255 255 / 0.24), transparent 72%),
      linear-gradient(180deg, transparent 0%, var(--wash) 28%, var(--wash-deep) 62%, transparent 100%);
  }
  .part-head {
    padding-block: 2.5rem 1rem;
  }
  .part-num {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--part-label-color, color-mix(in srgb, var(--tone) 75%, var(--ink)));
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .part-head h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 1.8rem + 3vw, 4.4rem);
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .part-tag {
    position: sticky;
    top: 4.75rem;
    z-index: 5;
    height: 0;
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline: var(--gutter, 1.5rem);
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .part-tag span {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.85rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tone) 70%, var(--ink));
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--page-paper) 80%, var(--tone));
    box-shadow: 0 4px 14px -6px rgb(27 34 84 / 0.25);
  }
  #part-bibliography {
    padding-bottom: 0;
    background:
      radial-gradient(ellipse 50% 70% at var(--glow-x) var(--glow-y), rgb(255 255 255 / var(--glow)), transparent 72%),
      linear-gradient(180deg, var(--part-stop-1) var(--part-p1), var(--part-stop-2) var(--part-p2), var(--part-stop-3) var(--part-p3), rgb(255 244 248) 30rem, #fff 40rem, #fff 100%);
  }
  .big-quote {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
  }
  .big-quote p {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.4rem + 2.6vw, 3.6rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    font-weight: 300;
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }
  .big-quote cite {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  .section-head {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .facet-grid {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .facet-grid-static { align-items: stretch; }
  .facet-grid-two { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
